  "notes": [
    {
      "cell": "Receipts (£bn)!C40",
      "text": "Outturn fiscal data consistent with the ONS/HM Treasury Public Sector Finances Statistical Bulletin released on 22 September 2026."
    },
    {
      "cell": "Receipts (£bn)!C41",
      "text": "Forecast years from 2026-27 are consistent with the OBR Economic and fiscal outlook forecast published October 2026."
    },
    {
      "cell": "Receipts (£bn)!C42",
      "text": "1 Includes road lorry user charge. 2 Also includes capacity markets, green gas levy and warm home discount. 3 Also includes, residential property developers tax and Pillar 2 taxes"
    },
    {
      "cell": "Glossary!C5",
      "text": "Revenue relating to activities in the current year, comprising mainly direct and indirect taxes, but also including social security contributions, interest, dividends, capital taxes and profits from trading activities."
    },
    {
      "cell": "Glossary!C6",
      "text": "The sum of public sector current expenditure, public sector net investment and public sector depreciation. "
    },
    {
      "cell": "Glossary!C11",
      "text": "The difference between total public sector receipts and expenditure on an accrued basis each year. As the widest measure of borrowing it is a key indicator of the fiscal position. PSNB is the headline measure of 'the deficit'."
    },
    {
      "cell": "Glossary!C14",
      "text": "A stock measure of the public sector's net liability position i.e. its liabilities minus its liquid assets. It is broadly the stock equivalent of public sector net borrowing, but measured on a cash rather than an accrued basis. It is also the fiscal measure used for the Government's supplementary fiscal target. PSND is the key measure of the country's overall debt."
    },
    {
      "cell": "Glossary!C15",
      "text": "A measure of the amount of cash which is needed to make up the difference betweeen spending and revenues. This is different from net borrowing as it also includes financial transactions, which will affect the cash position but are not included in net borrowing. Public sector net debt is driven by changes in the public sector net cash requirement."
    }
  ],
